      "side": 1,
      "message": "Placing `\u003c[^\u003e\\n]+\u003e` inside the repeating `(?:...)+` group alongside `[^()\\n]` in `LINK_TARGET_PATTERN` causes two issues in this commit (resolved when switching to `Lexer.lexInline` in change 643703):\n\n1. **Over-matching across multiple links on the same line**: In CommonMark, an angle-bracketed destination (`\u003c...\u003e`) is only valid at the start of `(...)`. Because `\u003c[^\u003e\\n]+\u003e` can match at any position inside the `+` loop, a `\u003c` inside a link title and a `\u003e` in a subsequent link on the same line (e.g., `[Link 1](https://example.com/1 \"a \u003c b\") and [Link 2](https://example.com/2 \"c \u003e d\")`) matches `\u003c[^\u003e\\n]+\u003e` across the closing `)` and fuses both links into a single token.\n2. **Catastrophic backtracking (ReDoS)**: Because `\u003c[^\u003e\\n]+\u003e` and `[^()\\n]` match overlapping character sets inside `(?:...)+`, an unclosed link target containing repeated `\u003c...\u003e` segments (e.g., `\"[a](\" + \"\u003cx\u003e\".repeat(25)`) triggers exponential $O(2^k)$ backtracking.\n\nLifting the angle-bracketed destination out of the `+` loop avoids both:\n```ts\nconst LINK_TARGET_PATTERN \u003d\n  \u0027(?:\\\\s*\u003c[^\u003e\\\\n]+\u003e(?:\\\\s+[^()\\\\n]*)?|(?:\\\\([^()\\\\n]*\\\\)|[^()\\\\n])+)\u0027;\n```\n\n_from potto_",
